/* book exam traps: The oxygenator is an artificial lung, not only an oxygen adder. It must remove carbon dioxide too; if CO₂ builds up, blood pH falls, which the perfusionist sees on the blood gas results. | Do not set a fixed 5 L/min for every adult. Flow is indexed to body surface area, 2.2–2.4 L/min/m², so a small adult gets less flow. | A roller pump is occlusive and can build pressure against a blocked line; a centrifugal pump is non-occlusive and can run dry. They need different alarms and limits. | MAP during bypass is about 50–80 mmHg, lower than the usual non-bypass value of about 70–100 mmHg. A low reading can be normal for bypass. | Heparin is given before bypass and reversed with protamine; it is not the cardioplegia drug. Cardioplegia is a cold potassium-rich solution given through a separate line. */
